 ### [GPU Mesh Voxelization via Plane/Projection Tests](6744-gpu-mesh-voxelization-via-plane-projection-tests.md)
-This method converts a triangle mesh into a solid voxel grid by testing each voxel within a triangle's bounding box against the triangle's plane equation and its 2D orthogonal projections. It differs from its sibling [Sparse Voxel DAG Voxelization via Bounding Box Grid and Plane Projections](1680-sparse-voxel-dag-voxelization-via-bounding-box-grid-and-plan.md) by using a dense bit-packed voxel table and explicit 3D plane tests rather than a hierarchical DAG structure, and from [Surface Voxelization](1683-surface-voxelization.md) by filling the interior of the mesh volume rather than marking only surface-adjacent cells.
+This method converts a triangle mesh into a solid 3D voxel grid on the GPU by explicitly testing every voxel within a triangle's bounding box against the triangle's plane equation and its 2D projections on the XY, YZ, and ZX planes. It is distinguished from its sibling [GPU Voxelization via Projection and Bitmasking](13349-gpu-voxelization-via-projection-and-bitmasking.md) by using full 3D plane and projection tests for every voxel rather than a compact bitmask per voxel to store depth occupancy, making it suitable for general solid voxelization where explicit geometric containment is required.
+
+### [GPU-based Voxelization via Compute Shader](17936-gpu-based-voxelization-via-compute-shader.md)
+A technique to convert 3D triangle meshes into a voxel grid representation using a GPU compute shader. It solves the problem of generating a 3D boolean occupancy grid from mesh geometry by offloading the rasterization or projection logic to the GPU. Unlike CPU-based voxelization, this method leverages parallel compute shaders to mark occupied cells in a global grid buffer.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:**
-    *   Variant A (SAT): $O(V \cdot T)$ where $V$ is the number of voxels and $T$ is the number of triangles [S2, S3, unofficial].
-    *   Variant B (Plane/Projection): $O(T \cdot V_{bbox})$ where $V_{bbox}$ is the number of voxels in the triangle's bounding box [S1, S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(G_x G_y G_z)$ for the voxel table [S1, S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Supports resolutions up to $256^3$ with real-time performance (10 fps or higher) for models with up to 2 million triangles [S6].
-*   **Strengths:** Exact geometric results; no rasterization artifacts; suitable for both surface and solid voxelization [S3, S4, unofficial][S6].
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high triangle counts due to brute-force intersection testing; requires explicit triangle-voxel logic [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When exact geometric intersection is required, or when rasterization-based methods are unsuitable (e.g., non-polygonal inputs, specific occupancy criteria) [S3, unofficial][S6].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For very high-poly meshes where $O(V \cdot T)$ becomes prohibitive; consider spatial acceleration structures [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(T \cdot V_{local})$, where $T$ is the number of triangles and $V_{local}$ is the average number of voxels in a triangle's bounding box. The brute-force triangle-by-voxel approach is $O(V \cdot T)$ in the worst case without spatial acceleration [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(G^3 / 32)$ for the bit-packed voxel table, plus $O(T)$ for triangle data and $O(N_{occupied})$ for the output coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Supports resolutions up to $256^3$ with real-time performance (10 fps or higher) for models with up to 2 million triangles [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Exact geometric containment; no rasterization artifacts; supports surface and solid variants (with optional propagation pass) [S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high triangle counts due to lack of spatial acceleration structures like BVH or grids [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When exact surface coverage is required and the mesh triangle count is moderate, or when rasterization-based methods are unsuitable [S1, unofficial][S7, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For very high-poly meshes where $O(V \cdot T)$ becomes prohibitive, or when approximate voxelization is sufficient [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Introduced in 2010 as a real-time voxelization technique for complex models [S6]. It leverages compute shaders (Direct3D 11, Shader Model 5.0) to perform triangle-voxel intersection tests on the GPU, avoiding the bandwidth demands of frame buffer access [S4, unofficial][S6]. The method has been implemented in various open-source projects and Unity assets, often referred to as "GPU Voxelizer Part 3" or "Compute Shader Voxelization" [S2, S4, unofficial]. It is used in applications such as volume-based modeling, transparent rendering, and collision detection [S6].
+GPU Mesh Voxelization was introduced by M. Schwarz and H.P. Seidel in 2010 in the paper "Fast Parallel Surface and Solid Voxelization on GPU's" [S6]. It is used in applications including volume-based modelling, transparent rendering, and collision detection [S7, unofficial].
 
